FS-06 of the Floor Studio series.

The route, the client experience, the chrome, the sitemap, the markdown
twin and the registry primitive. Everything that decides an answer still
lives in lib/floor-studio, where it is pure and tested; this is the
sequence a person moves through:

  photo → what we measured (and what you fix) → how you want it to feel →
  three real floors in your room → change anything → the range → the exit

THE INTERACTIVE PART IS AN ISLAND IN A SERVER PAGE

The wow is a canvas and a canvas needs a browser. Everything around it —
what the studio is, which floors exist, what the bands are, what it
refuses to do — is server-rendered prose, because a page whose content
appears only after hydration is a page a crawler, a retrieval system and
somebody on a bad connection all see as empty. This site paid for that
lesson once already (F-160: <form> count zero on every page).

THE CORRECTION IS A CONTROL, NOT AN ERROR PATH

Four corners, draggable and keyboard-operable with arrow keys, present
from the moment the analysis finishes whether it went well or not. The
person looking at the screen can see their own floor and we cannot.

A CONTROL THAT CANNOT BE CHOSEN SAYS WHY

Pick smoked hard maple and the studio explains the tannin; widen a
herringbone past five inches and it explains the block cut, moves the axis
the visitor did NOT just touch, and narrates what it moved. A disabled
button with no explanation teaches a visitor the product is broken.

WHERE IT SITS IN THE FUNNEL

The homepage band now leads with "Don't imagine your new floor. See it."
and keeps the configurator as the second link for the minority who arrive
knowing their specification. Floor Studio leads the Start here column of
the header menu — depth 0, one click from every page — and sits in the
footer beside the designer. /design links across to it.

And the studio funnel deliberately does NOT point at the estimate form.
Somebody who has just watched a floor appear in a photo of their own
living room has resolved desire, not doubt; the next uncertainty is
whether the picture was honest, and the answer to that is finished houses.
The page carries its own estimate CTA at the wow moment.

THE MONEY LADDER IS DECLARED AND NOT PUBLISHED

content/constants/studio-products.ts holds all three rungs — samples, the
personal floor plan, the professional design review — with priceCad: null
and published: false on the two paid ones. A new dollar figure is a Class
C change and no engineer gets to make it in a patch. The studio renders
nothing at all for an unpublished rung: no price, no "from", no "coming
soon" that could read as an offer. A test asserts every rung's price is
still null and that only the free one ships.

MACHINE SURFACES

/floor-studio.md states the capability, the vocabulary and the four
refusals. `action:visualise_floor` joins the registry as a ViewAction —
the third category of action this site has: work a machine cannot do FOR
somebody, only send them to, because it needs their photograph and their
device. The refusals are the citable part: an assistant choosing between
visualisers has no way to tell which ones invent products, and the one
that says in a machine-readable document that it renders only installable
configurations is the one worth naming.

16 wiring tests. 465 across the app. pnpm verify green (66 guards).

